              <!--  Product Overview  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">Product Overview</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Retatrutide is an investigational research peptide supplied for laboratory and in-vitro research applications. Also identified by the development code LY3437943, retatrutide is a synthetic molecule designed to activate three hormone receptors: GIP, GLP-1 and glucagon.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">The compound has attracted considerable scientific interest because of its simultaneous activity across these three metabolic pathways. Research has investigated retatrutide in relation to glucose regulation, energy metabolism, body-weight regulation and other metabolic processes.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Retatrutide remains an investigational compound and should be distinguished from medicines that have already received regulatory approval.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">For laboratory and in-vitro research only. Not intended for human or veterinary use. Retatrutide is not presented as a licensed medicine.</p>
              </section>

              <!--  What Is Retatrutide?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">What Is Retatrutide?</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Retatrutide, also known as LY3437943, is a synthetic peptide-based triple hormone-receptor agonist developed to activate the receptors for glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide (GIP), glucagon-like peptide-1 (GLP-1) and glucagon.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Unlike conventional therapies that target one or two of these receptors, retatrutide has been designed to engage all three pathways through a single molecule. This has made it an important subject of research into metabolic regulation and energy homeostasis.</p>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4">Clinical and laboratory research has examined retatrutide in relation to glucose metabolism, insulin secretion, glucagon signalling, energy expenditure, body-weight regulation, appetite and metabolic control, lipid metabolism, glycaemic control, cardiometabolic research, and liver and metabolic disease models.</p>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">GIP Receptor</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Glucose-dependent insulinotropic polypeptide, commonly abbreviated to GIP, is an incretin hormone involved in glucose-dependent insulin secretion and broader metabolic signalling. Activation of the GIP receptor has therefore become an important area of metabolic research.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">GLP-1 Receptor</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Glucagon-like peptide-1, or GLP-1, is another incretin hormone involved in glucose regulation, insulin secretion and appetite-related signalling. GLP-1 receptor activation is already an established pharmacological research pathway and forms one component of retatrutide's mechanism.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Glucagon Receptor</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">The third component is the glucagon receptor. Glucagon has an important role in regulating energy availability and glucose metabolism, while also being investigated for its potential influence on energy expenditure and lipid metabolism. Retatrutide's simultaneous activity at these three receptors is the defining feature of the molecule. Research has therefore focused on whether combining these pathways within a single molecule can produce distinctive metabolic effects.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </section>

              <!--  How Is Retatrutide Investigated in Research?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">How Is Retatrutide Investigated in Research?</h2>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Triple-Receptor Activity</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-2">The principal research interest in retatrutide comes from its ability to activate three hormone receptors simultaneously. The molecule combines:</p>
                    <ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside flex flex-col gap-1 mb-2">
                      <li>GIP receptor activity</li>
                      <li>GLP-1 receptor activity</li>
                      <li>Glucagon receptor activity</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">This distinguishes retatrutide from single-receptor and dual-receptor agonists.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Glucose Regulation</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Research has investigated retatrutide's effects on glucose metabolism and glycaemic control. In a Phase 2 trial involving participants with type 2 diabetes, retatrutide produced dose-dependent reductions in HbA1c compared with placebo. More recent Phase 3 research has continued to investigate retatrutide as a treatment candidate for people with type 2 diabetes.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Body-Weight Regulation</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Body-weight change has been one of the most extensively investigated outcomes in retatrutide research. The Phase 2 obesity trial reported substantial, dose-dependent reductions in body weight over 48 weeks. More recently, Phase 3 research has continued to examine retatrutide in people with obesity and related metabolic conditions.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Energy Metabolism</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">The addition of glucagon-receptor activity to GIP and GLP-1 receptor activation has generated interest in retatrutide as a research tool for studying energy metabolism. Researchers have investigated how these pathways may interact to influence energy expenditure, substrate utilisation and overall metabolic regulation.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Lipid Metabolism</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Glucagon and incretin signalling are also relevant to lipid metabolism. Retatrutide research has therefore included investigation of changes in measures associated with metabolic health and lipid handling.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Appetite and Metabolic Signalling</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">GIP and GLP-1 pathways are associated with appetite and nutrient-response signalling. Research into retatrutide has consequently examined changes in appetite-related and metabolic outcomes alongside its effects on body weight and glucose regulation.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </section>

              <!--  Research Applications  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">Retatrutide Research Applications</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-3">Retatrutide has been investigated across a growing range of metabolic and physiological research areas. Research applications include:</p>
                <ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside flex flex-col gap-1 mb-3">
                  <li>Metabolic research: investigation of GIP, GLP-1 and glucagon signalling</li>
