              <!--  What Does the Research Say?  -->
              <section>
                <h2 class="text-xl font-bold mb-4 text-primary" style="font-family: var(--font-heading)">What Does the Research Say?</h2>
                <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-4">Retatrutide has progressed further through clinical research than many compounds commonly described as research peptides. It remains investigational, but published clinical trials have provided substantial evidence regarding its pharmacological activity and effects on metabolic endpoints.</p>
                <div class="flex flex-col gap-5">
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Phase 2 Obesity Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">A major Phase 2 randomised, double-blind, placebo-controlled trial published in the New England Journal of Medicine investigated retatrutide in adults with obesity or overweight accompanied by a weight-related condition. Participants received once-weekly retatrutide at several dose levels or placebo over 48 weeks. The study reported substantial dose-dependent reductions in body weight and identified gastrointestinal events such as nausea, diarrhoea, vomiting and constipation among the reported adverse events. The study provided important evidence supporting further investigation of triple GIP/GLP-1/glucagon receptor agonism.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Type 2 Diabetes Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">A separate Phase 2 trial examined retatrutide in adults with type 2 diabetes. The study found dose-dependent reductions in HbA1c and body weight, with the higher retatrutide doses producing greater changes in these measures. Gastrointestinal adverse events were among the most frequently reported treatment-emergent effects. These results helped inform subsequent clinical development.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Phase 3 Research</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Retatrutide has subsequently progressed into Phase 3 clinical development. A 2026 Phase 3 trial, TRANSCEND-T2D-1, evaluated once-weekly retatrutide in adults with type 2 diabetes inadequately controlled by diet and exercise. The study reported significant improvements in HbA1c and body weight across the studied retatrutide doses compared with placebo. Gastrointestinal adverse events were generally mild to moderate and tended to decrease over time. Separate Phase 3 programmes have also been designed to investigate retatrutide in obesity and related conditions, including obstructive sleep apnoea and knee osteoarthritis.</p>
                  </div>
                  <div>
                    <h3 class="text-base font-semibold mb-2 text-primary">Interpreting the Evidence</h3>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed mb-2">The current evidence base is considerably more developed than for many experimental peptides, but retatrutide remains an investigational compound. Researchers should distinguish between:</p>
                    <ul class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed list-disc list-inside flex flex-col gap-1 mb-2">
                      <li>Published clinical trial findings</li>
                      <li>Pre-clinical pharmacology</li>
                      <li>Regulatory approval</li>
                      <li>Commercial or anecdotal claims</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p class="text-sm text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">Clinical-trial results provide useful evidence regarding the molecule's pharmacological activity, but they do not mean that an unapproved research product is equivalent to a regulated pharmaceutical product.</p>
                  </div>
                </div>
              </section>

